但再次，我的意思是，它在某种程度上说明了林肯在很多方面，你知道，领先于传统思维一个多世纪甚至更多。他对那种手势的掌握是无与伦比的，我想。无论如何，他们到达了南方同盟的白宫。你去过那里吗，汤姆？

54
00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:29.000
No, I haven't. No. I have. It's quite interesting, actually. It's quite weird because it's a bit like the real White House, but just not as good. Right. Well, I mean, that's the Confederacy for you, isn't it? And he goes and he sits in Jefferson Davis's chair. He's exhausted.
不，我没有。没有。我去过。实际上挺有趣的。挺奇怪的，因为它有点像真正的白宫，只是没那么好。好吧，我的意思是，这就是南方同盟，不是吗？他走进去，坐在杰斐逊·戴维斯的椅子上。他筋疲力尽。

55
00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:58.000
He's very haggard. He's described by captains who are there that he looks, a tired man whose nerves had carried him beyond his strength. He asked for a glass of water, and then they say, would you like to go around all Jefferson Davis's apartments? And he says, no, no, I don't think that'd be right. It's not right to tour another man's home. Amar that? Yeah, exactly. And it ends with him giving this speech in Capital Square in Richmond to a generally black crowd, again freed slaves.
他非常憔悴。在场的船长形容他看起来像一个疲惫的人，他的神经把他推到了体力极限之外。他要了一杯水，然后他们说，你想参观杰斐逊·戴维斯的所有公寓吗？他说，不，不，我觉得这不太合适。参观别人的家是不对的。是吗？对，完全是。最后他在里士满的国会广场向基本上是黑人的群众（同样是获释的奴隶）发表了演讲。

56
00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:23.000
And he says to them, my poor friends, you are free, free as air. You can cast off the name of slave and trample upon it. It will come to you no more. Liberty is your birthright. God gave it to you as he gave it to others. it's a sin that you have been deprived of it for so many years. And then he ends by saying, you know, learn the laws, be good citizens, obey God's commandments, thank him for giving you liberty, for to him you owe all things.
他对他们说：“我可怜的朋友们，你们自由了，像空气一样自由。你们可以抛弃奴隶的名字并践踏它。它将不再找你们麻烦。自由是你们的天赋。上帝赐予了你们，正如他赐予其他人一样。你们被剥夺了这么多年，这是一种罪过。”然后他以这样的话结束：“去学习法律，做优秀的公民，遵守上帝的诫命，感谢他赋予你们自由，因为万物皆归于他。”

57
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:55.000
It's a very moving stuff. He stays in Richmond for another day, and then he tours the front, and then on the 9th of April, he returns to Washington, D.C. And his mood at this point is a kind of odd mixture. At times, he's very giddy almost with happiness, but then he can be. be very melancholy. So when they're sailing home on the River Queen, he actually reads a soliloquy from Macbeth to his, to his companions. Hard to imagine the current president doing that, I think. But it's so interesting, isn't it? Because it's, what is it, in your notes
这是非常感人的东西。他在里士满多呆了一天，然后参观了前线，然后在4月9日，他返回了华盛顿特区。他此时的心情是一种奇怪的混合。有时他几乎因幸福而头晕目眩，但有时他又会非常忧郁。所以当他们乘“河后”号船回家时，他实际上向他的同伴们朗读了《麦克白》中的独白。很难想象现任总统会这样做，我想。但这不是很趣吗，因为它是什么，在你的笔记里

58
00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:37.000
you say, it's the syllable queen Macbeth delivers after he's murdered Duncan. Yeah. So much blood. And that must be expressive of a sense of guilt. I mean, Macbeth is consumed by guilt. It's so interesting he would choose that speech. Because Macbeth is actually saying at one point, I'm so troubled by guilt that I envy Duncan, who I've killed, who is now sleeping under the sleep of the just. And I think Lincoln absolutely feels that. You know, he's on this tour of the front, he's said again and again how much he regrets the bloodshed and the killing. You know, he doesn't glory in victory. But it's more than that, isn't it? Surely it's a sense of, I mean, maybe unrealised even by himself, of worry whether he has been the
你说，这是麦克白在谋杀邓肯后发表的独白。是的。如此多的血。这一定表达了一种负罪感。我的意思是，麦克白被罪恶感吞噬了。他选择这段演讲真是太有趣了。因为麦克白实际上在某个时刻说，我被罪恶感困扰，以至于我嫉妒我杀死的邓肯，他现在正安睡在正义者的睡眠中。我想林肯绝对有这种感觉。你知道，他在前线巡视，一次又一次地表示他多么后悔流血和杀戮。你知道，他并不为胜利而感到荣耀。但这不仅如此，对吧？当然这是一种感觉，我的意思是，也许甚至连他自己都没有意识到，担心他是否一直是

59
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:55.000
the tyrant of Confederate invective, whether he has spilled the blood of Americans on the altar of his presidency? I don't know. No, I think you're right, Tom. I think everything we know about Lincoln suggests that he's a man who, while ultimately a very effective politician,
南方同盟抨击中的暴君，他是否在自己总统职位的祭坛上洒下了美国人的血？我不知道。不，我想你是对的，汤姆。我想我们对林肯的了解都表明他是一个这样的人：虽然最终是一个非常高效的政治家，

60
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:22.000
very firm, decisive, all of those things, that he is an intensely reflective person. And there is also a kind of humility to him. I mean, I don't want to turn into a kind of Lincoln, hagiography, but he is one of those characters in history that you kind of look for an opportunity to be revisionist and actually there isn't really one. Now, the one thing I will say is the thing about Macbeth, I think there is a fatalism to Lincoln at this point. He has said many times to people, I wonder if I will ever see the end of the war, whether I will live to
非常坚定、果断，所有这些，但他是一个极其内省的人。他身上也有某种谦逊。我的意思是，我不想变成某种林肯圣徒传，但他是历史上的那些人物之一，你有时会寻找机会去做修正主义者，但实际上并没有这样一个机会。现在，我要说的一件事是关于麦克白的事，我认为林肯此时有一种宿命论。他多次对人们说，我不知道我是否能看到战争的结束，我是否能活到

61
00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:56.000
see the end of it. He has dreams of his potential murder, doesn't he? Exactly so. Exactly. He said to Harriet Beecher Stowe, however it ends, I have the impression that I shan't last long after it is over. And actually, at this point, when he's reading that speech from Macbeth, it is the 9th of April and he has only five days to live. But actually, while he's on the boat, there is a very, very dramatic development. Elsewhere in Virginia, at Appomattox Courthouse, Robert E. Lee's army has been cornered and trapped. They failed to break through the Union lines.
看到它的结局。他梦到自己可能被暗杀，对吧？完全正确。千真万确。他对哈丽特·比彻·斯托说，无论结局如何，我有一种感觉，这一切结束后，我活不了多久了。而实际上，此时当他正在朗读《麦克白》中的那段台词时，已经是4月9日了，他只剩下五天可活。但实际上，就在他在船上时，发生了一个非常非常戏剧性的进展。在弗吉尼亚州的其他地方，在阿波马托克斯法院，罗伯特·E·李的军队已经被包围并陷入绝境。他们未能突破联邦军队的防线。

62
00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:41.000
And that afternoon, in a place called the McLean Farmhouse, Lee signs the instrument of surrender. It's a scene we described before, I think, in our Custer series. Yeah, because he was there, wasn't he? Yeah. Eli Parker, who was General Grant's adjutant, wrote out the document. And when Lee found out that this bloke was a Seneca Indian, he said, oh, it's good to have one real American here. And an absolute Hollywood style, Parker said, sir, we're all Americans. And everyone clasped their hand over the heart and burst into patriotic tears. Everybody cries, but nobody more than tabby watching that in the film version, I think. Anyway, the next day, the 10th, Lincoln is back in Washington. He's actually in great form. And his advisors say, you know, they'd never seen
那天下午，在一个叫麦克林农庄的地方，李将军签署了投降书。我想，这是我们在卡斯特系列中曾描述过的场景。是的，因为他在场，不是吗？是的。作为格兰特将军副官的伊利·帕克起草了这份文件。当李发现这家伙是个塞内卡印第安人时，他说，哦，这里能有一个真正的美国人真好。具有绝对的好莱坞风格的是，帕克说，先生，我们全都是美国人。每个人都把手放在胸前，热泪盈眶。每个人都在哭，但我猜，看电影版的塔比哭得比谁都厉害。不管怎样，第二天，即10日，林肯回到了华盛顿。他的状态其实非常好。他的顾问们说，你知道，他们从未见过

