00:28:41.000 --> 00:29:19.000
him so happy because he's heard the news. Because he's fun, isn't he as well? He's great sense of humour. So when he's, when he's on a jig, he's all in. Exactly. He's a very, he's an up and down person, I think it's fair to say. When he's melancholy, he's very melancholy and when he's jolly, he's very jolly. And of course, he's got big challenges now. Now they really have to make up their mind. What do they do with the rebel states? Now, at the time, lots of people say, why don't you give a speech? Go on everyone wants a speech. But he said, no, no, I need a day to think about it. So it's not until the next day, the 11th of April, that evening, with a huge crowd waiting outside the White House that he gives this speech. And very unusually, and this is a
他这么高兴，因为他听到了消息。因为他很有趣，不是吗？他很有幽默感。所以当他情绪高涨时，他整个人都投入进去了。完全正确。他是一个情绪起伏很大的人，我觉得这样说很公道。当他忧郁时，他非常忧郁；当他快乐时，他非常快乐。当然，他现在面临着巨大的挑战。现在他们必须下定决心了。他们该拿叛乱各州怎么办？当时，很多人说，你为什么不发表一篇演讲呢？去吧，每个人都想听演讲。但他说，不，不，我需要一天时间考虑一下。所以直到第二天，即4月11日那天傍晚，白宫外聚集了庞大的群众，他才发表了这篇演讲。非常罕见的是，这标志着

64
00:29:19.000 --> 00:30:01.000
sign, I think, of the political pressure, he reads from a text. He doesn't deliver his remarks extemporary. Because he needs every word to be finally valued and graded. He says, I'm going to talk about reconstruction, and sometimes I'm betrayed into saying things that other people don't like. And he doesn't talk really at all about, again, no triumphalism. He says, I would like us to welcome back the rebel states as though they were never out of the union. In other words, we're like loving parents and we're welcoming the errant child back into the family. Or the prodigal son, because I imagine that, you know, the language of the New Testament is saturating his prose. But now comes the kicker. For the very first time in public, Lincoln says,
我认为，这是政治压力的一个信号，他是照着稿子念的。他没有即兴发表演讲。因为他需要让每一个字都经过最终的权衡和打分。他说，我要谈谈重建，有时我会不经意地说出一些别人不喜欢的话。他几乎完全没有谈到任何凯旋主义。他说，我希望我们重新欢迎叛乱各州回来，就像他们从未离开过联邦一样。换句话说，我们就像慈爱的父母，正迎回迷途知返的孩子。或者说像浪子回头，因为我想，你知道，新约圣经的语言充斥着他的散文。但接下来才是重头戏。林肯破天荒第一次在公开场合表示，

65
00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:31.000
I think it is time to give the vote to some of our black fellow citizens. He says, I would myself prefer that it were now conferred on the very intelligent and on those who serve our cause as soldiers. Now, that may sound like only a small move, but that's how Lincoln always works. He works with the sort of thin end of the wedge. He's a gradualist, he's always very, exactly what he did with emancipation. He moves slowly but steadily towards a more radical position. This is a massive, massive moment.
我认为是时候把投票权赋予我们的一些黑人同胞了。他说，我个人更倾向于现在就赋予那些非常聪明的人，以及那些曾作为士兵为我们的事业服务过的人。这听起来可能只是一小步，但林肯一向是这样办事的。他采用的是一种渐进的方式。他是个渐进主义者，总是非常——这正是他在废奴问题上所做的。他缓慢但坚定地朝着更激进的立场迈进。这是一个无比重大的时刻。

66
00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:54.000
And for one listener in the crowd in particular, these words are like an electric shock or something. And this bloke is a 26-year-old actor. And when he hears Lincoln say these words, he turns to the man next to him in the crowd, and he says, that means citizenship. Now, by God, I'll put him through.
对于人群中的某一位听众来说，这些话就像是一记电击或是别的什么。这家伙是一个26岁的演员。当他听到林肯说出这些话时，他转向人群中身旁的一个人，说道，这意味着公民身份。现在，凭上帝起誓，我一定要干掉他。

